ts (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034ee953-8e42-5d4f-9ffd-643223680ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336061} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e659c-5d07-52c4-b526-23b3bfb65498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337010} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ee5674-04b9-5043-b281-83cb64765c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337542} {:answer/id #uuid "b5423d8f-866a-57fb-bf8c-161986bd620e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337645} {:answer/id #uuid "ba69c603-d9dc-5d2b-97b6-5045ea9697f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e659c-5d07-52c4-b526-23b3bfb65498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337010} :answer-field/id #uuid "81c3396a-b700-5d8b-a2a8-a611018f6a4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8018b9d7-b35d-54ca-b97a-1ac935d3c057" :question/math-academy-id "q-71815" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }2x+y<6,\\,0<x<3}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }1\\le x<3,\\,2<y\\le 4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}<6}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,4).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third set is *open *since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
